5. Deep copy on every read — the core cost of the "value semantics with explicit ^ aliasing" design. Every variable read, field access, and index (lines 828, 831, 908) does a full recursive copy() of arrays/structs, and every set/bind runs a full O(n) graph walk with fresh HashSets for cycle detection. Combined with per-element Cell allocation, arrays are expensive to build and to access. Fine for a scripting interpreter, but it's the root of both the crash above and most constant-factor slowness. A persistent/shared representation would help.
6. No tail calls and no recursion-depth guard. Recursion is marketed in the spec, but deep or infinite recursion overflows the stack and (with panic = "abort" in the release profile, Cargo.toml:18) aborts without any diagnostic. All the other failure modes get nice file:line:col errors; this one kills the process.
7. io.read reads one byte at a time (src/modules/io.rs:186) — a syscall per byte on unbuffered File. A BufReader would make line reads orders of magnitude faster in loops.
8. Global mutable state duplicated as bookkeeping. Five thread_local! slots including LAST_ERROR_SPAN, which is written on every eval node (line 818). That's thread-local borrow traffic on the hot path, and the "last span any node touched" scheme is fragile — an error in a native function points at whatever node happened to evaluate last, not necessarily the failing call.
9. _ is doing three jobs — null literal, regex gap marker, and the magic "callable descriptor" key that makes any struct containing a _ field callable via auto-unboxing (invoke_operator, line 1301). That last one is a surprising generalization of the module-descriptor mechanism onto every struct, and operator_value recursion on _ chains is yet another infinite-loop vector in the same family as bug #1.
10. Smaller nits: descriptor spec is re-validated on every module call (validate_descriptor → validate_descriptor_spec in the call path); HTTP failures are reported as IOError (layering); %f of a large integer converts through f64; use special-cases the AST shape (let …) instead of evaluating normally; ne is pairwise-distinct while eq is only adjacent-chained (asymmetry that surprises); +Inf is rejected while NaN/Inf/-Inf are literals; no shebang support despite it being a script runner.
